            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, for the age &amp; mechanical systems design of your house, it has an average amount of air leakage, however there are some key areas that we identified. The majority of the air leakage is associated with the combined 7" and 6" chimney for the current oil boiler and domestic hot water heater, and this chimney would be eliminated if you upgraded to a direct-vent combination heating &amp; hot water system. In addition, there is some air leakage associated with the exterior doors (which you are planning to replace), the door entering the garage (which you can upgrade the weather-stripping on), around the attic hatch (which can be sealed with a removable caulking) and the two pot lights in the top of the kitchen window. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 476&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;19.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 construction with R20 batt insulation. The wall adjoining the garage appears to be 2x4 construction with R12 batt insulation. You are planning to complete an exterior wall upgrade to this house, including the addition of 2" (R10) of extruded polystyrene foam insulation. I would recommend you add the additional insulation to 100% of the exterior walls, including the wall adjoining the garage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an additional R10 insulation to 100% of the exterior walls of your home, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 426&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

















</mainWalls>
                <exposedFloors>

The bedrooms on the second floor have an exposed floor to the garage below. I would estimate the exposed floor has 2x10 joists with R20 batt insulation (the same amount that was originally in your attic before you added more attic insulation). I have modelled increasing the insulation in the joists to R32, and adding another 2" (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation on the underside (ie: the ceiling of the garage) and covered with drywall.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding a minimum R20 to this exposed floor area, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 65&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 190 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

















</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation, and the header above has R12 batt insulation installed. The basement floor is assumed to have no insulation below the slab. I have modeled adding a minimum R24 to 100% of the walls and another R20 to 100% of the header space above. As there is lots of height in this basement, it would be ideal to add a minimum R8 - R10 of expanded polystyrene insulation above the slab &amp; beneath a floor covering.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding a minimum R24 to 100% of the walls, another R20 to 100% of the header space above, and R10 to the floor, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,042&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;43.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,375 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;b&gt;The upgrade recommendation for your domestic hot water is integrated into the boiler upgrade recommendation above.&lt;/b&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil-fired boiler is rated at 85% AFUE, and vents up a 7" flue. In addition, there is a separate oil fired hot water tank with a 6" flue, that co-vents together with the boiler up the chimney. There does not appear to be a combustion air supply for the boiler or hot water tank, so they both draw their combustion air from within the house (more about this discussed below). With the insulation blanket on the hot water tank, I could not determine the model, so I assumed its' capacity as 40 US gallons (151 litres) that usually has an Energy Factor (EF) of 0.53



During the assessment, we talked about higher efficiency direct vent systems (oil or propane), where the chimney could be eliminated and the heating system(s) would no longer rely on internal house air for combustion. The domestic hot water could also be heated from a zone off the boiler (a "combi loop"). An ENERGY STAR® direct vent, oil boiler would have about an 87% AFUE or only about 2% more than you current boiler. Using this boiler to also heat your hot water (including a 40 USG storage tank) would increase the hot water efficiency up to a 0.60 EF



As discussed, there are ENERGY STAR® direct vent, propane boilers with AFUE's of 95% and higher. When these are used to also heat your hot water (with a 40 USG storage tank), the hot water efficiency increases to 0.79 EF. I have modeled the savings associated with this design.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion, propane-fired boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, and using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (with a 40 USG storage tank), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;54.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air&lt;/b&gt;



There does not appear to be a combustion air supply for the boiler or hot water tank, so they both draw their combustion air from within the house. I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler and hot water tank are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front roof (facing slightly SE), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 942&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year in a hydro community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



















</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood is an internal recirculation model, so the exhaust fans include 2 bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.5 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home from your combustion appliances (boiler and hot water tank).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Water Conservation&lt;/b&gt;



There are 3 toilets in your house and the one in the master bedroom is an older unit that consumes alot of water. I recommend you replace this toilet with one that uses 4.8 Liters or less, or a dual-flush toilet. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



